/**
 * @file isolation.dox
 * @brief Theory-native feature-isolation overview.
 *
 * @page feature_isolation Feature Isolation Analysis
 *
 * The isolation layer operates on the theory-native feature registry derived
 * from the valid and available system spaces. It answers the question:
 *
 * "Which minimal system witnesses isolate this theory feature, or this class of
 * candidate features, from the other available valid systems?"
 *
 * @section iso_core_idea Core idea
 *
 * For a given feature ID, the active implementation partitions the available
 * valid systems into:
 *
 * - `I`: systems in which the feature is present,
 * - `U`: systems in which the feature is absent.
 *
 * From these sets it computes:
 *
 * - the intersection of feature IDs over `I`,
 * - the union of feature IDs over `U`,
 * - and the flattened difference
 *   \f$\bigcap I \setminus \bigcup U\f$.
 *
 * That flattened difference is the candidate feature set isolated by the
 * witness `(I, U)`.
 *
 * @section iso_result_model Result model
 *
 * `difference_t` stores one witness pair `(I, U)`.
 *
 * `isolation_result_t` stores:
 * - `features`: the isolated candidate feature set,
 * - `differences`: the set of minimal witness pairs that isolate exactly that
 *   candidate set.
 *
 * This is important in the missing-systems case: the isolated result may be a
 * set of multiple candidate features rather than a singleton. The visible report
 * therefore labels results as `UNIQUE` or `AMBIGUOUS`.
 *
 * @section iso_public_api Public API
 *
 * `feature_isolation_t` is the main entry point.
 *
 * Key member functions:
 *
 * - `partition(feature_id)`
 * - `sid_to_fids(system_id)`
 * - `flatten_by_intersection(sids)`
 * - `flatten_by_union(sids)`
 * - `flattened_difference(I, U)`
 * - `minimal_differences(feature_id)`
 * - `some_difference(feature_id)`
 * - `print(...)`
 * - `name(feature_id)`
 *
 * `search_minimal_differences(...)` is the dynamic witness-search helper used
 * by the current implementation. It no longer depends on a fixed compile-time
 * bitset size.
 *
 * @section iso_reporting Reporting
 *
 * The theory-native report prints these columns:
 *
 * - `FEATURE ID`
 * - `FEATURE NAME` or the simplified term
 * - `CANDIDATE FEATURES`
 * - `RESULT TYPE`
 * - witness information
 *
 * `RESULT TYPE` is `UNIQUE` when the isolated candidate set has size 1 and
 * `AMBIGUOUS` otherwise.
 *
 * @section iso_usage Typical usage
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * feature_isolation::feature_isolation_t isolation("example.spl");
 * isolation.print(std::cout);
 * @endcode
 *
 * or for a single feature:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * feature_isolation::feature_isolation_t isolation("example.spl");
 * isolation.print(std::cout, 1, true, true);
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section iso_limitations Limitations
 *
 * The current implementation still constructs the complete theory feature space
 * from the valid-system space. That is semantically correct but can become
 * combinatorially expensive for larger SPLs.
 */
